  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nStrong high pressure is forecast to remain anchored over\nsouthern Quebec thru midweek. Ensemble guidance is forecasting\nsea level pressure on the order of once in a decade or more in\nand around the St. Lawrence Seaway. This will keep the weather\nlocally dry, with a breezy northeast wind. It will also help to\nkeep mixing shallow and temps on the cool side for the latter\nhalf of September. Overnight I anticipate that we will get some\ngood radiational cooling and the usual valley fog that\naccompanies it. We will have to be mindful of frost however, as\nthe growing season remains active in the foothills.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nPotentially greater weather impacts are possible from a storm\ndeveloping off the Mid Atlantic coast which is forecast to track\nnortheast off the New England coast by the weekend.\n\nNow deterministic models have two very distinct camps, the GFS\nout to sea and the ECMWF with an early season nor'easter hit.\nHowever the ensemble guidance is rather on the same page. Both\nthe GEFS and ECMWF Ens have hits and misses at roughly equal\nrates. Eyeballing it as slightly more than 50 percent hits for\ncoastal precip. NBM guidance is sitting at low likely PoP for\nthe coast and parts of southern NH which seems entirely\nreasonable for this range of the forecast. Some of the higher\nend ECMWF Ens members (top 10 percent), as evidenced by the EFI\ntool, do show that the QPF and wind gusts could be significant \nfor this time of year. Also looking at ensemble sensitivity and\nthey point towards heights over the upper Midwest into the Great\nLakes over the next few days as being critical. Already 22.00z\nraobs show heights are higher than the previous GFS forecast, so\nI would not be surprised to see the 22.00z GFS run tick closer\nto the coast and closer the ECMWF solution. \n\nThe storm could bring a myriad of hazards though. Heavy rain is\na possibility with the right track. Northeast winds this time of\nyear already tend to be gusty, with cold advection over warm\nocean waters, but the pressure gradient will be strong\nregardless of storm track. A low end wind advisory is not out of\nthe question for coastal areas, and a reasonable worst case\nscenario (10 percent chance) could be a strong wind advisory and\nfurther inland. This will also help to build seas on the coastal\nwaters in excess of 10 ft. While the weather will not be\nstellar, it is still high surf season and a high surf advisory\n(including a higher risk for rip currents) may be necessary for\nthe weekend as well. With persistent NE flow and long wind \nfetch across the Gulf of Maine, water levels should build. This \nmeans that surge may need to be factored into a period \nencompassing the highest tides of the month.",
